<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<subsection xml:id="lec-tieneSentido" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

<shorttitle>Lección 17 </shorttitle>
<title>Lección 17 -<nbsp/></title>
<title>¿Tiene sentido?</title>


<!-- ============================================ 
  Objetivos de aprendizaje                        
=================================================  -->

<!-- Frase resumen para estudiante. Originalmente [Student Facing Goals] -->
<introduction component="est-goal"> 
  <p>Decidamos si nuestras respuestas tienen sentido.</p>
</introduction>

<!-- Objetivos para el estudiante. Originalmente [Student-Facing Targets] -->
<!-- <objectives component="estudiante">
  <ul>
    <li>[@@@@@@@@@@@@@@]</li>
  </ul>
</objectives> -->

<!-- Objetivos que solo ve el profesor. Originalmente [Teacher Facing Goals] -->
<objectives component="profesor">
  <ul>
    <li><p>[++++++++++++++++]</p></li>
    <li><p>Assess the reasonableness of answers using mental computation and estimation strategies including rounding.</p></li>
    <li><p>Solve two-step word problems using addition and subtraction in a way that makes sense to them.</p></li>
  </ul>
</objectives>


<!-- ============================================ 
  Introducción para el profesor                        
=================================================  -->
<introduction component="profesor">
<p>
<dl>
  <!-- Estándares CCSS asociados -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="ccss-leccion-titulo"/></title> 
    <ul>
      <li> 
        <custom ref="ccss-addressing"/>        
        <xref ref="ccss-3-OA-D-8" text="custom">3.OA.D.8</xref>
      </li>
      <li>
        <custom ref="ccss-buildingTowards"/>
        <xref ref="ccss-3-OA-D-9" text="custom">3.OA.D.9</xref>
      </li>
    </ul>
    </li>

  <!-- índice de la lección (automático) -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="timeline-leccion-titulo"/></title>
    <list-of elements="subsubsection reading-questions" scope="subsection"/>
  </li>

  <!-- Propósito de la lección -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="purpose-leccion-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p>The purpose of this lesson is for students to use mental computation and estimation strategies such as rounding to decide if answers to two-step word problems make sense.</p>
  </li>

  <!-- Materiales requeridos -->
  <!-- <li>
    <title><custom ref="materials-leccion-titulo"/></title>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Actividad 1</p>
        <xi:include href="./act-estimacionesRapidas-mat.ptx"/>
        <xi:include href="./act-estimacionesRapidas-matCentros.ptx"/>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Actividad 2</p>
        <xi:include href="./act-resolverYRazonar-mat.ptx"/>
        <xi:include href="./act-resolverYRazonar-matCentros.ptx"/>
      </li>
    </ul>  
  </li> -->
  
  <!-- Introducción de la lección (narrative) -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="narrative-leccion-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p>Previously, students extended their understanding of addition and subtraction within 1,000 and learned how to round to the nearest ten and hundred. In this lesson, students work with two-step word problems and decide if a given answer for a two-step problem is reasonable. Students estimate answers to two-step problems and determine if each other's solutions make sense after they solve two-step word problems in a way that makes sense to them.</p>
  </li>
  
  <!-- Preguntas de reflexión profesor -->
  <li>
    <title><custom ref="reflection-quest-titulo"/></title>
    <p>[+++++++++++++++]</p>
    <p>Reflect on students’ assessment. Are they able to flexibly use different strategies, such as considering the answer in context, rounding, or estimating? How can you leverage their previous experiences with sense making and rounding to help them keep reasonableness in mind?</p>
  </li>
</dl>
</p>
</introduction>


